Lab Setup
For this lab, we'll use:

Server System: 192.168.1.100 (logserver.example.com)
Client System: 192.168.1.101 (client.example.com)
Task 1: Install and Configure rsyslog
Subtask 1.1: Install rsyslog on Both Systems
On both server and client systems:

# For RHEL/CentOS:
sudo dnf install -y rsyslog

# For Ubuntu/Debian:
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y rsyslog
Expected Outcome:
Rsyslog package is installed without errors.

Troubleshooting Tip:
If you get package not found errors, ensure your system is registered/subscribed (for RHEL) or has correct repositories (for Ubuntu).

Subtask 1.2: Configure rsyslog Server
On the server system (192.168.1.100):

Edit the rsyslog configuration file:
sudo vi /etc/rsyslog.conf
Uncomment or add these lines:
module(load="imudp")
input(type="imudp" port="514")

module(load="imtcp")
input(type="imtcp" port="514")

$template RemoteLogs,"/var/log/remotehost/%HOST-NAME%/%PROGRAMNAME%.log"
*.* ?RemoteLogs
& ~
Create the log storage directory:
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/remotehost
Restart rsyslog and enable firewall:
sudo systemctl restart rsyslog
sudo systemctl enable rsyslog

# For firewalld (RHEL/CentOS):
sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=514/tcp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=514/udp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# For ufw (Ubuntu):
sudo ufw allow 514/tcp
sudo ufw allow 514/udp
Expected Outcome:
Rsyslog server is listening on TCP/UDP port 514 and ready to receive logs.

Task 2: Configure Client to Forward Logs
Subtask 2.1: Configure rsyslog Client
On the client system (192.168.1.101):

Edit the rsyslog configuration:
sudo vi /etc/rsyslog.conf
Add at the end of file:
*.* @@192.168.1.100:514  # TCP forwarding
# OR for UDP:
# *.* @192.168.1.100:514
Restart rsyslog:
sudo systemctl restart rsyslog
Expected Outcome:
Client will begin forwarding all system logs to the central server.

Subtask 2.2: Test Log Forwarding
On client system:

logger "This is a test message from client"
On server system:

sudo tail -f /var/log/remotehost/client.example.com/logger.log
Expected Outcome:
You should see the test message appear in the server's log file.

Task 3: Analyze Centralized Logs
Subtask 3.1: Using journalctl (if using journald)
On server system:

journalctl -f
Expected Outcome:
Live view of system journal including forwarded logs (if journald is configured to receive syslog).

Subtask 3.2: Using rsyslog Log Files
On server system:

# View logs from specific client
sudo ls /var/log/remotehost/client.example.com/

# Search for specific logs
sudo grep "error" /var/log/remotehost/client.example.com/*.log
Expected Outcome:
You can see organized logs from all clients in their respective directories.

Advanced Configuration (Optional)
Filter Specific Logs
To forward only specific logs from client:

# In client's /etc/rsyslog.conf
authpriv.* @@192.168.1.100:514
*.error @@192.168.1.100:514
Encrypted Log Transfer
For secure log transfer:

On both systems:
sudo dnf install -y rsyslog-gnutls  # or apt-get equivalent
Configure TLS in rsyslog configuration
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Logs not appearing on server:

Verify network connectivity: telnet 192.168.1.100 514
Check rsyslog status: sudo systemctl status rsyslog
View rsyslog own logs: sudo tail -f /var/log/messages or /var/log/syslog
Permission issues:

Ensure /var/log/remotehost has proper permissions
SELinux might block access: sudo setenforce 0 to test
